+    // CONVERGENCE SKEW -- and the ONE place this port must NOT copy the
+    // flagship. The skew wants clip_x += s * view_depth. The flagship writes
+    // that as s * (dot(v,fwd) - dot(eye,fwd)), which is correct for OoT
+    // because OoT feeds Fast3D a COMBINED viewing*projection matrix, so the
+    // vertices reaching P are WORLD-ABSOLUTE and the camera position has to
+    // be subtracted back out.
+    //
+    // Banjo-Kazooie is the opposite convention: it renders CAMERA-RELATIVE.
+    // viewport_setRenderPerspectiveMatrix (core1/viewport.c) loads perspective
+    // and MULs roll/pitch/yaw into G_MTX_PROJECTION -- rotation only, no camera
+    // translation -- and then LOADS an identity modelview. The camera offset is
+    // baked per object instead: modelRender.c calls func_80252AF0(cameraPos,
+    // objectPos, ...) which translates by (objectPos - cameraPos)
+    // (core1/mlmtx.c:526-538). So the coordinates entering P are ALREADY
+    // camera-relative and dot(v,fwd) alone IS the view depth.
+    //
+    // Subtracting dot(eye,fwd) here therefore adds a constant clip-x offset of
+    // -s*dot(eye,fwd), with OPPOSITE SIGN PER EYE, proportional to the camera's
+    // ABSOLUTE world position -- and BK levels span +/-16000 units. At shipped
+    // defaults that is 3% of screen width at 500 units from the origin and 33%
+    // at 5000. Nothing near that can fuse; the two images rival and the viewer
+    // sees objects snapping between two positions. Device symptom, three
+    // builds running: "flickers between left right left right".
+    //
+    // The camera-SHIFT half above needs no such correction: a translation
+    // along right is translation-invariant, so it is already right for
+    // camera-relative input.
+    out[0][0] += s * gSoh3DCamFwd[0];
+    out[1][0] += s * gSoh3DCamFwd[1];
+    out[2][0] += s * gSoh3DCamFwd[2];
 }
